  -->

<chapter>

<title>Porting and Integrating Seminole</title>

 <section><title>Introduction</title>

  <para>
   Seminole is useable as a standalone webserver, but its primary
   purpose is to serve as one component of a whole embedded system.
   To that end, Seminole is provided with considerable support
   structure intended to ease its integration into an existing project
   environment.  In particular, Seminole has:

   <itemizedlist>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      A generalized, modular build system
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      A clear separation between portable and non-portable code
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </itemizedlist>

   As a result of these attributes, implementors will hopefully have
   more time to spend on their real goals rather than struggle with
   integration problems.
  </para>

  <para>
   This section discusses the Seminole build system and portability
   layers in further detail. 
  </para>

 </section>

 <section><title>The Seminole Build System</title>

  <section><title>Overview</title>

   <para>
    Like the rest of Seminole, the build system is oriented towards
    embedded systems developers. Using the build system in Seminole is
    completely optional.  If your project has a radically different
    build system from what Seminole offers, it can be used instead
    with little effort. The major features of the Seminole build system
    are:
   </para>

   <itemizedlist>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      The compilation tools and build environment are easily changed.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Multiple targets are easily selected and built.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      The built files for each target are isolated so one can have 
      Seminole built for a variety of targets at the same time.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </itemizedlist>

   <para>
    The Seminole build system uses portable make files that are
    generated by a Perl script. These makefiles recursively descend the
    directory tree and build Seminole for a specific target. The
    results of the build can then be used for linking into a larger
    embedded systems project in any way that is convenient for the
    project.
   </para>

   <para>
    There are two main variables that configure the build system: the
    <quote>port</quote> and the <quote>target.</quote> The
    <quote>target</quote> is a particular runtime environment, for
    example, <acronym>POSIX</acronym>. The <quote>port</quote>
    determines the tools used to compile Seminole as well as a
    particular target.
   </para>

  </section>

  <section><title>Performing a Build</title>
   
   <para>
    The first step is to configure Seminole for a particular port and
    target.  This can be done in several ways, depending on your
    <command>make</command> program.  Most <trademark
    class=registered>UNIX</trademark>-like <command>make</command>
    programs allow variables to be overridden on the command line.
    The default values for the port and target are assigned in the
    <filename>mkdefs</filename> file located in the top-level
    directory.  The variables are named <varname>PORT</varname> and
    <varname>TARGET</varname>.  The file should be edited to reflect
    the appropriate target and port.  Valid combinations of
    <varname>PORT</varname> and <varname>TARGET</varname> are listed
    in <xref linkend="portingBuildConfigs">.
   </para>

   <table id="portingBuildConfigs" frame=top rowsep=0 tocentry=1>
   <title>Supported Build Configurations</title>
   <tgroup cols=3 colsep=1>
   <thead>
    <row>
     <entry><varname>PORT</varname></entry>
     <entry><varname>TARGET</varname></entry>
     <entry>Platform</entry>
    </row>
   </thead>
   <tbody>
    <row>
     <entry>Linux</entry>
     <entry><acronym>POSIX</acronym></entry>
     <entry><acronym>GNU</acronym>/Linux with <application
      class=software>GCC</application></entry>
    </row>
    <row>
     <entry>OpenBSD</entry>
     <entry><acronym>POSIX</acronym></entry>
     <entry>Open<acronym>BSD</acronym> with <application
     class=software>GCC</application></entry>
    </row>
    <row>
     <entry><productname class=registered>Solaris2</productname></entry>
     <entry><acronym>POSIX</acronym></entry>
     <entry><productname class=registered>Solaris 2</productname> with 
      <application class=software>GCC</application></entry>
    </row>
    <row>
     <entry><productname class=registered>Irix</productname></entry>
     <entry><acronym>POSIX</acronym></entry>
     <entry><productname class=registered>Irix</productname> with
       <application class=software>MIPS ProC</application></entry>
    </row>
    <row>
     <entry><productname class=registered>MSVC</productname></entry>
     <entry>Win32</entry>
     <entry><productname class=registered>Windows NT</productname> with
       <application class=software>Microsoft Visual C++</application></entry>
    </row>
    <row>
     <entry>Tornado_win32</entry>
     <entry>VxWorks</entry>
     <entry><productname class=registered>Tornado</productname> on 
       <productname class=registered>Windows NT</productname></entry>
    </row>
   </tbody>
   </tgroup>
   </table>

   <para>
    Other toolchains can be added by examining the files in the
    <filename class=directory>ports</filename> directory and creating
    new port description files. A new target is added by creating the
    appropriate system interface linkages in the <filename
    class=directory>src/targets</filename> directory.  A skeletal
    <productname class=registered>VxWorks</productname> target is
    provided but cannot be used <quote>out of the box</quote> because
    of the differences in the distributed versions of the <productname
    class=registered>VxWorks</productname> environment.
   </para>

   <para>
    After configuring <filename>mkdefs</filename>, the next step is to
    generate the real makefiles from the templates. To do this, issue
    the following <command>make</command> command (for most
    <command>make</command> programs):
   </para>

   <para>
    <userinput>make deps</userinput>
   </para>

   <para>
    This step only has to be performed when the
    <varname>PORT</varname> or <varname>TARGET</varname> settings are
    changed. To build Seminole, the default target of
    <quote>all</quote> should be used.  This is accomplished,
    typically, with the following command:
   </para>

   <para>
    <userinput>make</userinput>
   </para>

   <note>
    <para>
    For a <varname>PORT</varname> of <symbol>MSVC</symbol>, the make command
    should be written as follows:
    </para>
    <para>
      <userinput>nmake /f Makefile.win32 <replaceable>targets</replaceable></userinput>
    </para>
    
    <para>
    It is recommended that your <varname>PATHEXT</varname> environmental
    variable also contain the suffix <screen>.pl</screen> so that the
    various perl tools (such as <xref linkend="toolSCPG">) can be invoked
    without needing their extension.
    </para>

   </note>

   <para>
    When complete, the results will be in <filename
    class=directory>built/$(PORT)-$(TARGET)</filename>.  The header
    files needed by the client application are placed in <filename
    class=directory>include</filename> and the libraries needed are
    placed in <filename class=directory>lib</filename>.  In addition,
    most ports provide a standalone binary that can be used to verify
    the build of Seminole.
   </para>

   <para>
    After building Seminole for the first time, one should be verify
    that the resulting libraries are functional.  When targetting a
    <acronym>POSIX</acronym> (or more generally, <trademark
    class=registered>UNIX</trademark>-like) target, this can be done by
    invoking the Seminole executable.
   </para>

   <para>
    The <filename>bin/shttpd</filename> program takes at a minimum of
    two arguments.  The first argument is the name of the server. A
    port number can optionally be specified with a
    <quote><literal>:</literal></quote> following the server name (for
    example,
    <quote><literal>toaster.example.net:8080</literal></quote>) Each
    additional argument represents a mapping from
    <acronym>URL</acronym> namespace to filesystem namespace.  For
    example, assuming <filename
    class=directory>/content/public</filename> is a valid directory,
    an argument of <quote><literal>/=sys:/content/public</literal></quote>
    would map the root of the Seminole webserver onto the files within
    <quote><filename
    class=directory>/content/public</filename></quote>.  Additional
    mappings can follow the first one.
   </para>

   <para>
    Mappings that begin with the <literal>sys:</literal> prefix are mapped
    to the native file system of the operating system. Another option is
    to use the <literal>pkg:</literal> prefix. This associates the filename 
    following <literal>pkg:</literal> with being a ROM filesystem image
    (generated with the SCPG tool).
   </para>

   <example><title>Starting the Seminole Executable</title>
<screen>shttpd toaster.example.net:8080 /=pkg:/content/public.dat /logs=sys:/var/logs</screen>
   </example>

   <para>
    If no path mappings are specified (just a hostname and port) then 
    the <filename>shttpd</filename> looks for a file called 
    <filename>semweb.pkg</filename> in its current working directory. If found
    this file is then mapped as the main web content. The build system builds
    an example <filename>semweb.pkg</filename> in the 
    <filename class=directory>bin</filename> directory from example content.
   </para>

   <para>
    Once proper operation is verified with a web browser, you have
    successfully built Seminole.
   </para>

   <note>
    <para>
    On Win32, Seminole will not work properly unless the path after the 
    <literal>sys:</literal> contains an absolute path and drive letter. 
    It is acceptable (and recommended) to use the forward slash 
    <literal>/</literal> as a path separator under Win32.
    </para>
   </note>

  </section>

 </section>

 <section id="portingOpEnv">
 <title>Operating Environment Abstraction Layers</title>

  <section><title>Introduction</title>

   <para>
    Seminole is designed so as to provide a clear division between
    portable and non-portable code.  Non-portable mechanisms are
    abstracted into particular classes to insulate the rest of
    Seminole from them.  A reference implementation of the
    non-portable classes is included with the Seminole distribution,
    and is suitable for <acronym>POSIX</acronym> systems with or
    without threads.  A template implementation for <productname
    class=registered>VxWorks</productname> is included, but requires
    some degree of modification to produce a functional whole.  In
    total, the following classes compose the non-portable parts of
    Seminole:

    <itemizedlist>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       <classname>HttpdOpSys</classname>
      </para>
     </listitem>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       <classname>HttpdDirectory</classname>
      </para>
     </listitem>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       <classname>HttpdFile</classname>
      </para>
     </listitem>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       <classname>HttpdFileInfo</classname>
      </para>
     </listitem>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       <classname>HttpdSocket</classname>
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </itemizedlist>

   </para>

   <para>
    One type of operating system service abstracted herein is likely to
    be of particular interest to embedded systems programmers: memory
    management.  Since all such services are obtained through the
    <classname>HttpdOpSys</classname> class, it provides a convenient
    bottleneck at which to constrain memory usage, if desired.
    Similarly, thread creation overruns can be managed at the source,
    or any other limited resource in need of controls or limits.
   </para>

   <para>
    Another place where operating systems differ widely is in their 
    support of networking models.  Within Seminole, these differences are
    nominally abstracted within <classname>HttpdSocket</classname>,
    but its public semantics do resemble those of
    <acronym>BSD</acronym> sockets.  While it's conceivable that a
    given network <acronym>API</acronym> could be so foreign to the
    current interfaces as to make a faithful implementation of
    <classname>HttpdSocket</classname> impossible, hopefully the
    general case will be simpler.  On any platform offering the
    sockets interface, porting the provided
    <classname>HttpdSocket</classname> implementation ought to require
    little or no modification.  Other interfaces supporting at least
    the general concepts of addressable communications endpoints and
    bi-directional data flow should be capable of abstraction within
    <classname>HttpdSocket</classname>.  If a given plaform's
    interface does not support byte-oriented <acronym>I/O</acronym>,
    some adapation will be required in the
    <function>ReadN()</function> and <function>WriteN()</function>
    methods.
   </para>

  </section>

  <section><title>Adding New Abstraction Layers</title>

   <para>
    The process of porting Seminole to a new target or host platform
    generally involves one or both of the following steps: 
   </para>

   <itemizedlist>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Create a new build framework definition file in <filename
      class=directory>ports/</filename>, named after the host
      environment, and populate it with appropriate
      <command>make</command> meta-commands and variables to carry out
      the Seminole build process.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Implement native versions of abstraction layer classes and
      associated glue, located in a directory named after the new
      target (resident within <filename
      class=directory>src/targets</filename>).  These consist of
      <classname>HttpdOpSys</classname>, the file I/O classes
      (<classname>HttpdFile</classname>,
      <classname>HttpdFileInfo</classname>, and
      <classname>HttpdDirectory</classname>), and the network
      abstraction layer, <classname>HttpdSocket</classname>.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </itemizedlist>

   <para>
    However, this process is not nearly as daunting as it may sound.
    Customization of the build environment is discussed earlier in
    this chapter; this section will concentrate on the second of the
    tasks mentioned above, the adaptation of Seminole's
    <acronym>OS</acronym> abstraction layer to a new type of target.
   </para>

   <para>
    Seminole is shipped with three pre-existing abstraction layers, one
    for <acronym>POSIX</acronym>-like systems, one for Win32, and one for
    <productname class=registered>VxWorks</productname>.  These layers can
    be copied and adapted to new targets, perhaps with very little
    modification necessary (depending of course upon the architecture
    in question).  Unless one is intimately familiar with the existing
    target implementations and thus has the confidence to start from
    scratch, it is highly advised to work from a template.    
   </para>

   <para>
    Essentially, the job of the abstraction layer implementor is to
    rewrite the classes mentioned earlier in this section, keeping to
    the public interfaces published in the <citetitle
    pubwork="book">Programmer's Manual</citetitle>.  The work involved
    in porting each method is highly dependent on the nature of the
    new target and what services it offers to applications.
   </para>

   <para>
    Other than the interfaces provided by the abstraction layer
    objects listed previously, a number of C preprocessor definitions
    exist which serve to activate certain extra features (to conserve
    code space when necessary) or to take into account capabilities
    offered by the target architecture.  In some cases one will need
    to adjust these definitions depending on services offered by the
    new <acronym>OS</acronym> abstraction layer.  The currently used
    definitions, and where they ought to be set, are listed in <xref
    linkend="portingPreprocSymbols">.  Alternatively, preprocessor
    symbols can be defined as compiler arguments in the
    <varname>CFLAGS</varname> variable of the <filename
    class=directory>ports/</filename> file in question (e.g.,
    <literal>-DHTTPD_INC_MODIFIED_SINCE</literal>).
   </para>
   
   <table id="portingPreprocSymbols" frame=top rowsep=0 tocentry=1>
   <title>Common Preprocessor Symbols</title>
   <tgroup cols=3 colsep=1>
   <thead>
    <row>
     <entry>Symbol</entry>
     <entry>Location</entry>
     <entry>Meaning</entry>
    </row>
   </thead>
   <tbody>
    <row>
     <entry><symbol>HTTPD_HAVE_REENTRANT_LIB</symbol></entry>
     <entry><filename>src/targets/<replaceable>target-name</replaceable>/sem_sys.h</filename></entry>
     <entry>Defined if using a reentrant C library</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
     <entry><symbol>HTTPD_INC_MODIFIED_SINCE</symbol></entry>
     <entry><filename>src/seminole.h</filename></entry>
     <entry>Defined if <literal>If-Modified-Since:</literal> header
      support is desired</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
     <entry><symbol>HTTPD_INC_PERSISTENT_CONN</symbol></entry>
     <entry><filename>src/seminole.h</filename></entry>
     <entry>Define this to include support for persistent connections.</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
     <entry><symbol>HTTPD_INC_DIRECTORY_LISTS</symbol></entry>
     <entry><filename>src/seminole.h</filename></entry>
     <entry>Defined when directory indexing support is desired</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
     <entry><symbol>HTTPD_INC_LZRW1KH_COMPRESSION</symbol></entry>
     <entry><filename>src/seminole.h</filename></entry>
     <entry>Defined when the LZRW1/KH (codepoint <literal>1</literal>) 
            is to be supported by the ROM file system</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
     <entry><symbol>HTTPD_INC_LZARI_COMPRESSION</symbol></entry>
     <entry><filename>src/seminole.h</filename></entry>
     <entry>Defined when the LZARI (codepoint <literal>2</literal>)
            is to be supported by the ROM file system</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
     <entry><symbol>HTTPD_AVOID_LINGER</symbol></entry>
     <entry><filename>ports/PortName</filename></entry>
     <entry>Define if <symbol>SO_LINGER</symbol> does not work
            correctly on your targets TCP/IP stack</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
     <entry><symbol>HTTPD_MAX_HEAP_USAGE</symbol></entry>
     <entry><filename>ports/PortName</filename></entry>
     <entry>If defined this places a hard limit on the amount
            of memory Seminole will use. Attempts to keep
            more than this amount (in bytes) allocated will 
            result in memory allocation failures that Seminole
            will handle gracefully. This limit is enforced in the
            <classname>HttpdOpSys</classname> interface which may
            be user supplied (and thus, not posess this feature).</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry><symbol>HTTPD_MAX_THREAD_USAGE</symbol></entry>
      <entry><filename>ports/PortName</filename></entry>
      <entry>If defined this places a hard limit on the number of
             threads that Seminole will spawn. This limit is enforced in
             the <classname>HttpdOpSys</classname> interface which may
             be user supplied (and thus, not posess this feature).</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry><symbol>HTTPD_MAX_WAIT_FREE_TASK</symbol></entry>
      <entry><filename>ports/PortName</filename></entry>
      <entry>If defined, <symbol>HTTPD_MAX_WAIT_FREE_TASK_INCR</symbol> 
             and <symbol>HTTPD_MAX_THREAD_USAGE</symbol> must
             also be defined. If both these symbols are defined then 
             Seminole will wait for up to 
             <symbol>HTTPD_MAX_WAIT_FREE_TASK</symbol> milliseconds (in 
             increments of <symbol>HTTPD_MAX_WAIT_FREE_TASK_INCR</symbol>
             milliseconds) for the number of running threads to go below
             <symbol>HTTPD_MAX_THREAD_USAGE</symbol>. 
             
             This limit is enforced in the <classname>HttpdOpSys</classname>
             interface which may be user supplied (and thus, not posess 
             this mechanism).</entry>
    </row>
   </tbody>
   </tgroup>
   </table>

   <para>
    Additionally, the provided <acronym>POSIX</acronym> target layer
    makes use of a few preprocessor symbols which may be relevant in
    adapting it to new <acronym>POSIX</acronym> variants.  These are
    listed in <xref linkend="portingPOSIXPreProc">, and may be set in
    <filename>src/targets/POSIX/sem_sys.h</filename>, or in a
    <filename class=directory>ports/</filename> file as described previously.
   </para>

   <table id="portingPOSIXPreProc" frame=top rowsep=0 tocentry=1>
   <title><acronym>POSIX</acronym> Preprocessor Symbols</title>
   <tgroup cols=2 colsep=1>
   <thead>
    <row>
     <entry>Symbol</entry>
     <entry>Meaning</entry>
    </row>
   </thead>
   <tbody>
    <row>
     <entry><symbol>HTTPD_USE_FORK</symbol></entry>
     <entry>Define this to use <function>fork()</function> rather than
      <acronym>POSIX</acronym> threads</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
     <entry><symbol>HTTPD_USE_SINGLE</symbol></entry>
     <entry>Define this to cause Seminole to have only one thread of
     execution; each <function>Fork()</function>'ed function runs to
     completion from the caller. Mostly this is used for debugging.</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
     <entry><symbol>HTTPD_USE_POLL</symbol></entry>
     <entry>The <function>poll()</function> system call is far more 
            efficient than the <function>select()</function> system
            call. If your system supports <function>poll()</function>
            then this symbol should be defined to make use of it.</entry>
    </row>
   </tbody>
   </tgroup>
   </table>

   <para>
   Seminole also has some parameters that, in the interest of efficiency,
   are defined at compile time in <filename>src/seminole.h</filename>. 
   The defaults are normally adequate although they may be changed for special
   circumstances. 

   <table frame=top rowsep=0 tocentry=1>
   <title>Compile-time constants</title>
   <tgroup cols=4 colsep=1>
   <thead>
    <row>
     <entry>Symbol</entry>
     <entry>Meaning</entry>
     <entry>Units</entry>
     <entry>Default Value</entry>
    </row>
   </thead>
   <tbody>
    <row>
     <entry><symbol>HTTPD_MAX_MIME_ENTRIES</symbol></entry>
     <entry>The maximum number of name-value pairs on an incoming
            request.</entry>
     <entry>Object count</entry>
     <entry>48</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
     <entry><symbol>HTTPD_MAX_INPUT_LINE</symbol></entry>
     <entry>The maximum lenght of a line on an incoming request.</entry>
     <entry>Byte count</entry>
     <entry>512</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
     <entry><symbol>HTTPD_XFER_BUF_SIZE</symbol></entry>
     <entry>The size of a transfer buffer used when sending content
            to the client.</entry>
     <entry>Byte count</entry>
     <entry>128</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
     <entry><symbol>HTTPD_SINK_BUFFER_SIZE</symbol></entry>
     <entry>The size of the data buffers that
            <xref linkEnd="HttpdContentSink"> uses to keep track of
            dynamically generated content.</entry>
     <entry>Byte count</entry>
     <entry>512</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
     <entry><symbol>HTTPD_FIRST_TIMEOUT</symbol></entry>
     <entry>The amount of time to wait after the establishment of a
            TCP connection for the first line of the first request.
            This timeout serves two purposes. First, it serves to prevent
            persistent connections from lasting indefinitely. Second, it
            functions as a denial-of-service attack recovery 
            mechanism.</entry>
     <entry>Seconds</entry>
     <entry>160</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
     <entry><symbol>HTTPD_MIME_TIMEOUT</symbol></entry>
     <entry>The amount of time to wait after a request line is received
            for the transmission of the MIME name-value pairs. Unlike
            <symbol>HTTPD_FIRST_TIMEOUT</symbol> expiration of this timer
            would more likely indicate a network or protocol error rather
            than an overloading condition.</entry>
     <entry>Seconds</entry>
     <entry>30</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
     <entry><symbol>HTTPD_HCLOSE_TIMEOUT</symbol></entry>
     <entry>This is the amount of time to allow the TCP connections managed
            by Seminole to exist in the <symbol>FIN_WAIT_2</symbol> state.
            </entry>
     <entry>Seconds</entry>
     <entry>3600</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
     <entry><symbol>HTTPD_MAX_REQUESTS_PER_CONN</symbol></entry>
     <entry>This is the maximum number of requests that can be processed on
            a single TCP connection. Even if the 
            <symbol>HTTPD_FIRST_TIMEOUT</symbol> is not exceeded the maximum
            number of requests can never be exceeded.</entry>
     <entry>Request count</entry>
     <entry>5</entry>
    </row>
   </tbody>
   </tgroup>
   </table>

   </para>

  </section>

 </section>

</chapter>
